# Email verification for new login devices

Users can enable `User.enable_new_device_email_verification` in their profile.
It defaults to false. A correct password then requires an email code when the
account has a previous successful device login, the current device has no usable
known-device cookie, and TOTP/passkey authentication is not active. Revoked or
expired device records still count as successful login history.

The email goes exclusively to `User.email`, regardless of optional notification
preferences, contact roles, or template recipient overrides. Login codes and
rendered messages use the normal administrator-only mail history and transaction
inspection. Challenge options contain a bcrypt hash, not a plaintext code.

Verification messages have plain-text and HTML versions. Both use the service,
request time, readable device description, IP address and reverse DNS record
from the initiating login attempt, including when resending the code.

Challenges last 30 minutes. Resend replaces the code without extending that
deadline or resetting failed attempts. Each challenge permits five wrong codes,
three sends with a 60-second cooldown, and there may be three pending challenges
per account. Database buckets limit accounts to five sends and ten wrong guesses
per 15 minutes, plus 20 sends per UTC day. Source IP buckets allow 60 sends and
100 wrong guesses per 15 minutes. Buckets use fixed UTC intervals.

Password-based token requests return `complete: false`, an opaque continuation
`token`, `valid_to`, and `next_action: email_code`. Submit that token and the
protected string `code` to the advertised `email_code` action. The `email_resend`
action accepts the same token and returns a pending continuation. Requested
scope, lifetime, and interval remain bound to the original request. A required
password change follows successful email verification with a fresh five-minute
`reset_password` continuation. HTTP Basic rejects these accounts when email
verification applies and never sends mail. Existing supplied tokens, sessions,
refresh tokens, and SSO retain their existing behavior.

The Ruby HaveAPI CLI and PHP client support the advertised continuation actions.
The Terraform provider itself uses a supplied token, but its separate `get-token`
helper and current Go client only support TOTP and password-reset prompts. They
fail with `Unsupported authentication action 'email_code'` when email verification
is required. Use the HaveAPI CLI to obtain a token for these clients, or keep
using an existing token. Updating the Go helper is separate client work; account
enrollment must not imply that this helper supports email verification.

## Rollout

1. Apply the additive database migration. Keep the enrollment gate off.
2. Install the `user_login_email_verification` template and both languages.
   Built-in templates use `rake vpsadmin:notification_templates:install_defaults`.
   Managed installations reconcile the updated notification-template package;
   vpsFree.cz provides it in `vpsfree-notification-templates`.
   When updating an installation that already sends verification mail, update
   all API workers before installing templates that use the additional login
   details. New workers continue to support the earlier text-only templates.
   To roll back that update, restore the earlier templates first, then roll back
   the API workers. Older workers cannot render the additional login details.
3. Update every API worker handling OAuth2, token issuance, and HTTP Basic, then
   update WebUI. Older workers ignore the preference and must not handle logins
   for opted-in users.
4. Enable the core sysconfig key
   `new_device_email_verification_available`. This exposes profile enrollment.
   Turning the gate off prevents new opt-ins; it does not disable protection
   for existing opted-in accounts.

Self-service preference changes require the current password, including when an
administrator edits their own account. An administrator can override the
preference for another user; the change is recorded in the user audit history.
Enabling does not send a mailbox-confirmation email. Missing or disabled mail
templates prevent the email login from proceeding.

Password, primary-email, MFA, login-method, and account-restriction changes
invalidate pending authentication. OAuth challenges bind the original browser,
client, redirect URI, scope, state, and PKCE parameters. Device trust and
bootstrap exceptions are checked again before authority is issued.

The existing authentication housekeeping task removes expired challenges and
rate buckets. Keep the additive schema during application rollback. Older API
versions can read existing ordinary state but cannot enforce this preference;
a downgrade requires draining pending email logins and explicitly accepting the
loss of enforcement. No node or vpsAdminOS update is needed.
